imud (1.9.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * SPI transport for the IMU and the magnetometer. [imu] and [mag] each
    gain bus, spi_dev and spi_speed_hz, and choose independently.  Default
    is bus = "i2c"; existing configs are unaffected.
  * New magnetometer driver rm3100 (PNI RM3100): magneto-inductive, no
    SET/RESET coil, I2C or SPI, 1-600 Hz. Experimental until validated on
    hardware.
  * Higher sample rates: ism330dhcx and lsm6dso gain 3332 and 6664 Hz,
    icm42688p 4000 through 32000 Hz. These are rates the datasheets always
    specified and the drivers had never offered.  6664 Hz needs SPI;
    icm42688p above 8000 Hz will not run on a Raspberry Pi.
  * The manual installs as an Info page: info imud.  Building from source
    now requires texinfo.
  * Sample latency is instrumented. The [stats] line splits it into FIFO
    residence, which your fifo_wm and rate decide, and the daemon's own
    pipeline.
  * imud-status says when heading is dead-reckoned. Without a magnetometer
    calibration the filter's mag update never runs and heading drifts on
    the gyro alone, which nothing in the output used to show.
  * Sample rates are carried in milli-Hz throughout, so the advertised
    rates are now the silicon's own. The ISM330DHCX ladder is 6664/2^n,
    making its bottom rung 13.016 Hz where the datasheet prints 12.5.
    odr_hz accepts fractional rates. A rate that rounds to zero is
    refused.
  * imud --replay exits when the capture runs out, after letting the last
    samples reach the filter. It used to sit there until killed.
  * Replaying one capture twice now produces identical filter output, and
    sim_speed decides the pace rather than the reader threads' hardware
    poll cadences.
  * A reader thread that gives up stops the daemon instead of leaving it
    running with a dead sensor. A magnetometer that stops delivering is
    reported once per outage, and again when it recovers.
  * [imu] spi_speed_hz below 2.5 MHz warns at startup when the
    magnetometer shares the same SPI controller, naming the rate to use.
    Below that the magnetometer can stop measuring while still answering
    the bus.
  * Heave and sea state accumulate in double, so a long run no longer
    loses resolution in the integrators.
  * Calibration was applied in the wrong frame when [mount] was not
    identity: the daemon rotated board to body first and applied the
    sensor-frame calibration second.  Only affects a rotated mount; with
    [mount] identity the two frames coincide.  ACTION: Re-run imud-cal for
    any sensor on a non-identity mount.
  * The daemon could not start on arm64 or armhf under its own systemd
    unit, and installing or upgrading could leave a Raspberry Pi 5 unable
    to open its GPIO chip.
  * Several sample-timestamp defects: chip_ts could run backwards between
    bursts, a corrupted read could stamp a whole burst seconds into the
    future, the timer period was trusted rather than measured, and
    timestamps carried two offsets.
  * The magnetometer ran at a third of its configured rate, and the
    MMC5983MA lost its X axis over SPI when a CTRL0 write also cleared an
    inhibit bit.
  * imud-cal mag could write a soft-iron scale the swing never measured,
    stretching an axis to cover data that was never taken. An axis must
    now be swept through at least 0.8 of the fitted radius, capping any
    correction at 1.25x.
  * An MPU FIFO overflow desynchronised every later read: the bytes the
    part drops to make room are never a whole number of sample-sets, and
    this FIFO carries no tag to resync from.

 -- Richard Simpson <richcreations@gmail.com>  Mon, 10 Aug 2026 04:48:20 -0700

imud (1.8.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a bad value in imud.conf is now fatal, where it
    was previously accepted with no message at any log level. A value
    too long for its field was truncated and used — a 130-character
    [stream] socket became a different, perfectly valid 107-character
    path, and the daemon bound and served that while every bridge and
    libimud client connected to the path written in the config file and
    found nothing there. An out-of-range integer wrapped: dest_port =
    4294977414 landed as port 10118, odr_hz = 4294968129 as 833 — both
    positive, both plausible, neither written by the operator. And nan,
    inf and 1e999 converted cleanly into any float key. All three now
    refuse to start with the offending line named, and a SIGHUP reload
    keeps the running config. 18 integer keys also gained semantic
    bounds — ports 1..65535, int_gpio 0..255, i2c_addr 0x00..0x7F —
    because dest_port = 70000 fits an int perfectly well and then
    becomes port 4464 the moment htons() truncates it.
  * BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: imud --config PATH now means that file and no
    other. The $HOME/.config/imud/imud.conf fallback ran whether or not
    --config was given, so `imud --config /misspelled` silently started
    on someone else's config — while imud(8) has documented --config as
    replacing the search path since the beginning. A missing PATH now
    means built-in defaults, with a warning. A daemon started WITHOUT
    --config still searches /etc/imud/imud.conf then $HOME.
  * Fix: a non-finite calibration poisoned the filter permanently. A
    cal.json holding {"gyro":{"bias":[nan,0,0]}} loaded without
    complaint, and w = gyro - bias was then non-finite on every predict
    step. Normalisation could not repair it — NaN fails the n > 1e-10
    test, so the quaternion passed through untouched — and nothing
    downstream re-aligns, so it never washed out: 1000 perfectly level,
    noiseless samples later the published roll, pitch, yaw and
    quaternion were still all NaN, rendered as the literal "nan" into an
    NMEA sentence. The loader now rejects it and names the field, and
    the MEKF sanitises its own state once per fusion loop as a backstop.
    See the imud-utils changelog for the imud-cal half.
  * New flag bit FLAG_STATE_RESET (bit 14): the MEKF found a non-finite
    value in its own state and reset itself. Latched from the reset
    until the filter re-converges rather than a single-packet pulse, so
    a consumer polling at 1 Hz can still see it; FLAG_FUSION_CONVERGED
    is clear for the same span. No wire-format change — the packet stays
    v17 / 276 bytes and a reserved bit simply gained a meaning. Bit 15
    is now the last spare.
  * Fix: SIGHUP reload did nothing but fail on a daemon started from the
    $HOME config. Reload re-read the path the daemon was asked for
    rather than the one it had actually read, so a daemon that came up
    on the fallback answered every `systemctl reload` with "config
    reload failed" — re-reading a file it had never opened, and naming
    no path at all. A packaged install always has /etc/imud/imud.conf,
    so this bit source installs; reload messages now name the file.
  * Fix: deleting a key from imud.conf and reloading kept the old value.
    Reload seeded from the running config rather than from the defaults,
    so removing [nmea] rate_hz to get the default back left the previous
    rate in place while the daemon logged "config reloaded", and the
    next restart then behaved differently from the reload before it.
    Reload is now identical to a restart for the hot-reloadable fields.
    A config file that has been DELETED still keeps the running config,
    deliberately unlike startup: a file removed out from under a running
    daemon must not silently revert it.
  * Fix: a failed output thread left its socket bound with nothing
    behind it. Every listener is bound before any thread exists, so a
    pthread_create failure left a socket up that nobody would ever
    accept() from — a client connected successfully, into the backlog,
    and then waited forever. Losing the binary stream is now fatal,
    because it is the output the five bridges and every libimud consumer
    read, so a daemon without it produces nothing while systemd reports
    it active; the unit is Restart=on-failure with RestartSec=3, so that
    is a retry three seconds later. The opt-in nmea, hirate and position
    threads still warn and carry on, but now close what they had bound,
    so a client gets ECONNREFUSED rather than a connection that never
    answers. READY=1 now means the threads are running, not merely that
    the sockets are bound.
  * Fix: imud.service forbade the gpiochip imud.conf asked for. The
    shipped config had gpio_chip = "gpiochip4", correct for a Pi 5,
    while the shipped unit allowed only /dev/gpiochip0 under
    DevicePolicy=closed with the gpiochip4 line commented out — so no
    combination of the two files as shipped was correct on any board.
    The unit now lists all four supported nodes and the "comment out the
    other one" instruction is gone: an allow for a node the board does
    not have is inert, so one unit serves Pi 4 and Pi 5 alike.
  * No wire-format change: the packet stays v17 / 276 bytes.

 -- Richard Simpson <richcreations@gmail.com>  Wed, 05 Aug 2026 12:00:00 -0700

imud (1.8.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: imud did not start on stock Debian. The unit hardcoded
    Group=gpio, but nothing ever created the gpio or i2c groups —
    Raspberry Pi OS ships them, plain bookworm/trixie ships neither, and
    systemd refuses to start a unit whose groups cannot be resolved, so
    `systemctl start imud` failed with 216/GROUP. Both groups are now
    created if absent, on every install rather than only when the user is
    first created, so an upgrade repairs a host that was missing them.
    New udev rule 60-imud.rules grants them access to /dev/i2c-* and
    /dev/gpiochip*, numbered 60 so Pi OS's own 99-com.rules still wins.
  * BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: the daemon runs with imud as its primary group and
    keeps gpio and i2c as supplementary groups; the stream socket,
    /run/imud and /var/lib/imud are now imud:imud. Anything that read the
    stream socket or ran imud-status by virtue of gpio membership must
    join imud instead — on Pi OS the default user is in gpio, so
    imud-status reports the daemon unreachable until
    `sudo adduser "$USER" imud`. Deliberate: reading telemetry should not
    require a group that also grants raw GPIO and I2C access.
  * Fix: one configured ODR now means one rate everywhere. The filter was
    tuned from a rate rounded to nearest while every register-table
    driver programs the chip rounding up, so [imu] odr_hz = 900 tuned the
    MEKF for 833 Hz while the ISM330DHCX ran at 1660. The magnetometer
    rate was never snapped at all — [mag] odr_hz = 137 sized Rm for
    137 Hz while the MMC5983MA sampled at 200. The driver is now the
    authority: the rate is resolved once, up front, and the resolved
    value reaches the chip and the filter's noise model alike. Startup
    logs "N Hz requested, M Hz actual" when they differ. Numerically
    unchanged with the shipped defaults (833 and 100 are already on their
    grids); the wave benchmark is bit-identical.
  * BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: odr_hz and the seven rate_hz keys now reject 0 and
    negative values. [mag] odr_hz = 0 produced Rm = 0 and hence a
    magnetometer Kalman gain of exactly 1. A config setting any of them
    non-positive started before and now refuses to start.
  * Fix: a config file that exists but cannot be read is now a fatal
    error instead of falling through to defaults. A missing file still
    means defaults, as documented.
  * Harden: tier-2 systemd sandboxing — empty CapabilityBoundingSet,
    SystemCallArchitectures=native, a @system-service syscall allow-list,
    RestrictNamespaces, LockPersonality, RestrictSUIDSGID,
    ProtectHostname, ProtectProc=invisible, ProcSubset=pid and UMask=0027
    (0027 rather than 0077 so imud-group members can still tail
    /var/log/imud.log). ProtectClock= is deliberately absent and
    adjtimex(2) re-allowed, so the startup CLOCK_TAI check keeps working;
    setting the clock is already impossible without CAP_SYS_TIME. The
    reasoning is recorded in the unit file itself.
  * Harden: the AF_UNIX stream and status sockets are born 0660 rather
    than created at 0777 & ~umask and chmod'd a moment later, and a
    failed chmod is now fatal instead of ignored.
  * Harden: every fd is close-on-exec. Accepted sockets were the real
    gap — POSIX does not carry FD_CLOEXEC across accept(), so every
    NMEA/high-rate TCP client, every stream subscriber and every
    imud-status connection was inheritable on Linux.
  * Fix: `make install DESTDIR=<root>` no longer captures a local
    config/cal.json into the packaging root. A real install is unchanged.
  * Fix: the log level is now atomic. SIGHUP writes it from the signal
    thread while every other thread reads it on each log call, which was
    a data race by the C memory model.
  * No wire-format change: the packet stays v17 / 276 bytes.

 -- Richard Simpson <richcreations@gmail.com>  Mon, 03 Aug 2026 12:00:00 -0700

imud (1.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New tool imud-imutest ships in the imud-utils package: validates a
    sensor driver against real hardware and writes a Markdown report to
    attach to an issue. Ten of imud's twelve drivers have never run on
    physical silicon; a clean report is what clears an experimental flag.
  * New drivers mpu9250 / mpu9255 (IMU) and ak8963 (magnetometer) for the
    TDK InvenSense MPU-925x 9-axis breakouts, all experimental. The
    magnetometer answers at 0x0C through the IMU's I2C bypass.
  * Fix: the IMU chip timer's declared period was trusted rather than
    measured. The ISM330DHCX on the reference board runs 4.08% fast, so
    ts_wall_ns / ts_tai_ns ran about 2.4 s ahead over each 60 s anchor
    epoch and every per-sample dt handed to the filter was 4% long. The
    anchor now measures the counter's real period against CLOCK_MONOTONIC
    and uses that, rejecting measurements more than 10% from nominal.
  * Fix: imud-cal accel printed the wrong physical instructions for all
    six positions, so the fit silently fell back to an identity scale.
    Re-run it; a calibration saved by an earlier release does nothing.
  * Fix: imud-cal mag over-stated the soft-iron correction on any board
    with hard iron — the per-axis extent seeded its min/max from a raw
    sample and then compared centered ones against it. Re-run it.
  * Files created by the daemon and the tools (PID file, cal.json, .imucap
    captures, imutest reports) now carry an explicit 0644 rather than
    0666 narrowed by whatever umask the process was launched with.
  * Build: the tree is warning-clean again on gcc, and the clang static
    analyzer reports no bugs.

 -- Richard Simpson <richcreations@gmail.com>  Fri, 31 Jul 2026 22:00:00 -0700

imud (1.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * WIRE FORMAT v17 (packet 260 -> 276 bytes): four new MEKF diagnostic
    fields — innov_weight, innov_reject (how hard the filter is having to
    distrust its sensors) and nis_accel, nis_mag (whether the noise model
    itself is right; 1.0 = consistent). The daemon, all five bridges and
    imud-mon validate the wire version and must be upgraded together.
    libimud consumers do not need recompiling.
  * Fusion: the MEKF error state grows 6 -> 9 with a Gauss-Markov
    wave-acceleration state, so the seaway's disturbance of the gravity
    measurement is modelled instead of treated as white sensor noise. Two
    new hot-reloadable [fusion] keys tune it, mekf_wave_accel (default
    0.8 m/s^2) and mekf_wave_accel_tau_s (default 0.5 s); either at 0
    restores the previous 6-state filter exactly. Over the 12-seed wave
    benchmark: accel NIS 19.3/25.2 -> 1.01/0.69, NEES 18.3/7.8 ->
    3.47/0.99, attitude RMS 5.65 -> 4.45 deg (3-D), heading RMS 3.07 ->
    0.83 and 1.96 -> 1.02 deg. mekf_accel_noise now describes sensor
    noise only — rough weather is mekf_wave_accel's job.
  * Fusion: the gross-outlier rejection threshold moves 9x -> 25x the
    chi-squared gate. At 9x it discarded 26% of the benchmark's
    accelerometer updates — ordinary wave motion, not outliers.
  * Fusion: the error-state reset Jacobian G = I - 1/2[dtheta]x is now
    applied to the covariance after each correction is injected.
  * Fix: the innovation-covariance singularity test used an absolute
    determinant threshold (1e-12) on a matrix carrying physical units, so
    the accelerometer update was silently skipped once the filter
    converged — 87% of updates discarded in the wave benchmark. The test
    is now relative to the size of the matrix. Accuracy and consistency
    numbers in documentation predating this release were measured with
    the bug present and are not comparable.
  * Fix: initial attitude alignment averaged a hardcoded ~1 s of data —
    about a fifth of a roll period, so a daemon started underway aligned
    to an arbitrary point in the wave cycle and baked the tilt error into
    the magnetic reference's dip (47.7 deg attitude RMS at 1 s against
    2.19 deg at 5 s). The window is now [calibration] align_window_sec,
    default 5 s. Installs starting at a mooring were unaffected.
  * Fix: the gate health/consistency metrics averaged with a gain derived
    from the IMU sample rate, but the accel skip band discards 85-95% of
    samples in a seaway, so their true time constant was ~10 min rather
    than 30 s. The gain now comes from elapsed time.
  * New [fusion] key mekf_mag_dip_sigma_deg (default 1.0): the dip-angle
    uncertainty of the magnetic reference, admitted into the covariance
    as a rank-1 anisotropic noise term. Affects 3-D vector fusion only
    (mag_yaw_only = false); the marine yaw-only default is bit-for-bit
    unchanged. NEES-strict in 3-D mode improves 12.8 -> 5.7.
  * Config: mekf_gyro_noise, mekf_gyro_bias, mekf_accel_noise and
    mekf_mag_noise are rejected at load if zero or negative — each is a
    variance in a denominator, and 0 gave a Kalman gain of exactly 1.
  * Config, [mount]: an unrecognised preset name is now a fatal error
    rather than a warning that left a wrong rotation applied to every
    sample; rotation_euler_deg now requires exactly three values; new
    rotation_matrix key takes the board->body rotation as 9 row-major
    values, validated against R'R = I and det(R) = +1.
  * Engine-vibration detector: the EMA time constant is derived from the
    sample rate (a true ~1 s regardless of FIFO burst depth) and the
    threshold gained hysteresis, releasing at 70% of the assert level, so
    a boundary signal no longer chatters Ra_scale, the accel skip band,
    the log and the ENGINE_ON wire flag.
  * Fix: a config reload (SIGHUP) while the engine was running left the
    accel skip band narrow with noise inflation applied until the engine
    next stopped, and did not recompute the m_ref adaptation rate after a
    mag_odr_hz change.
  * New imud-cal fit-ra mode: replays a rough-water .imucap through the
    filter and reports the gravity-direction residual, its correlation
    time, the innovation-distance distribution against the gates, and the
    mean NIS. It writes nothing — what it comments on is filter tuning in
    imud.conf, not a sensor property. Two fixes over its first cut: the
    residual correlation time was low by the accel-skip decimation factor
    (3-10x), and it now calls the filter's own innovation code instead of
    a drifted copy, so the offline report and the live nis_accel field
    cannot disagree.
  * Flag bit 6 (FLAG_MOTION) is formally retired. It was defined but
    never set in any released version; the define stays so existing code
    compiles, but the bit will never be set or reused.
  * Docs: the Euler attitude fields (pitch, roll, yaw, heading_deg,
    rate_of_turn) are stated to be ZYX intrinsic angles, singular at
    pitch +/-90 deg by construction. Consumers that can encounter steep
    pitch must read quat_*. Nothing changed in the packet.
  * The rough-sea wave benchmark now averages over a fixed set of 12
    seeds instead of asserting on one unrepresentative draw, and the
    packet fuzz seed corpus is generated from packet_build() itself
    (make fuzz-seeds) with test_packet asserting it matches the wire.

 -- Richard Simpson <richcreations@gmail.com>  Mon, 27 Jul 2026 12:00:00 -0700

